      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127156,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127157,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127158,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> naevus lipomatosus cutaneous superficialis.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> naevus lipomatosus cutaneous superficialis of Hoffmann&ndash;Zurhelle.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127159,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Classic naevus lipomatosus superficialis; solitary naevus lipomatosus superficialis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127160,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Classic NLS is more common in the gluteal region and neighbouring skin {13858911; 4648802; 6629609; 7430482}, and it manifests with multiple lesions. There may be a linear or zosteriform distribution that does not cross the midline {1235780; 1219048}. Solitary NLS has a wider anatomical distribution, including in the head and neck {9248887; 16268886; 3273051; 5647029}, upper extremities {1235780; 22607282; 34494674; 31452824}, distal lower extremities {13858911; 1235780; 18474480}, and external genitalia {12974709}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127161,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>NLS manifests as slow-growing, skin-coloured to yellow, dome-shaped plaques formed by confluent papules and nodules. The overlying skin is wrinkled or cerebriform, sometimes with comedones {1235780; 1219048}. The plaques vary in size from 3&nbsp;mm to 200&nbsp;mm {13858911; 1219048}. There are usually no associated clinical abnormalities {13858911}. Rarely, NLS can be associated with generalized folded skin (Michelin tyre baby syndrome) {464627; 25424205}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127162,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>NLS is rare. In the classic form, lesions arise at birth or develop in the first two decades of life {1235780}. Solitary NLS has been reported in a wider age group (birth to 88&nbsp;years) {23113758; 12974709}. There is no sex predilection {17223884}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127163,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>NLS is thought to represent a malformation. Deletion of 2p24 has been reported in 1&nbsp;case {17223884}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127164,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is unknown. It has been suggested that lesional adipocytes arise from precursor cells lying close to the blood vessels, analogous to the sequence of events in fetal adipogenesis {7430482}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127165,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>NLS has a glistening, yellow to cream cut surface within the dermis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:39:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127166,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 223,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>NLS is composed of aggregates of mature adipocytes at the level of the papillary and upper reticular dermis, typically around blood vessels {13858911; 1219048}. Accompanying connective tissue changes in the dermis include increased dermal capillaries inversely proportional to the fat content, irregularly distributed and sometimes thickened collagen, reduction of elastic fibres, and increased numbers of fibroblasts {13858911; 11277956}. The epidermis may show irregular acanthosis, follicular plugging {13858911; 4648802; 1219048}, and (rarely) various abnormalities of folliculosebaceous units {12031036; 14721781; 16029355; 33223741; 15649197; 17224390}.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>NLS must be differentiated from lipoma and soft tissue fibroma with lipomatous stroma. Lipoma is typically a well-circumscribed subcutaneous nodular lesion composed mostly of mature adipocytes. Although thin connective tissue septa may separate lobules of adipocytes in lipomas, there is no dermis between the adipocytes like in NLS. Soft tissue fibroma contains groups of mature adipocytes in the stroma, but the lesion has a pedunculated silhouette. Soft tissue fibromas with fatty metaplasia are considered by some to be a solitary form of NLS.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-28T13:54:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
